            <ArticleTitle>Gene expression patterns in peripheral blood leukocytes in patients with recurrent ciguatera fish poisoning: Preliminary studies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>35-38</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1568-9883(16)30067-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hal.2016.03.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ciguatera fish poisoning (ciguatera) is a common clinical syndrome in areas where there is dependence on tropical reef fish for food. A subset of patients develops recurrent and, in some instances, chronic symptoms, which may result in substantial disability. To identify possible biomarkers for recurrent/chronic disease, and to explore correlations with immune gene expression, peripheral blood leukocyte gene expression in 10 ciguatera patients (7 recurrent and 3 acute) from the U.S. Virgin Islands, and 5 unexposed Florida controls were evaluated. Significant differences in gene expression were noted when comparing ciguatera patients and controls; however, it was not possible to differentiate between patients with acute and recurrent disease, possibly due to the small sample sizes involved.</AbstractText>
